=== PVC persistence test — Postgres pod deleted, PVC re-attached, data intact ===
Date: 2026-08-17

=== BEFORE: existing tasks ===
$ kubectl exec -it postgres-0 -n taskapp -- psql -U taskapp_admin -d taskapp -c "SELECT id, title FROM tasks;"

 id |           title            
----+----------------------------
  1 | Wash Cloth
  2 | checking argo
  3 | Testing Ingress and Egress
  4 | hello
(4 rows)

=== Deleting pod ===
$ kubectl delete pod postgres-0 -n taskapp
pod "postgres-0" deleted from taskapp namespace

=== Watching recovery ===
$ kubectl get pods -n taskapp -l app=postgres -w
NAME         READY   STATUS              RESTARTS   AGE
postgres-0   0/1     ContainerCreating   0          1s
postgres-0   0/1     ContainerCreating   0          1s
postgres-0   0/1     Running             0          1s
postgres-0   0/1     Running             0          6s
postgres-0   1/1     Running             0          6s

=== AFTER: same tasks still present ===
$ kubectl exec -it postgres-0 -n taskapp -- psql -U taskapp_admin -d taskapp -c "SELECT id, title FROM tasks;"

 id |           title            
----+----------------------------
  1 | Wash Cloth
  2 | checking argo
  3 | Testing Ingress and Egress
  4 | hello
(4 rows)

=== RESULT ===
Pod recreated in ~6 seconds, PVC re-attached automatically (StatefulSet +
volumeClaimTemplates), all 4 pre-existing rows present and unchanged after
recovery. No data loss.

CAVEAT (see ARCHITECTURE.md / RUNBOOK.md for full detail): this cluster's
default StorageClass is k3s's bundled `local-path`, which ties the PV to
the specific node it was first provisioned on rather than being a true
network-attached volume. This test proves recovery from a pod-level
deletion on the same node — it does not prove recovery from loss of that
node itself, which would require a network-attached StorageClass (e.g. the
AWS EBS CSI driver) to guarantee.